kind: instructions
title: "Task Files (only when a plan is split)"
summary: "Task files: an optional split of a plan's checklist across parallel scopes; the plan itself is the ledger."
---

`context/tasks/<YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS>-<slug-plan>.md` holds a **split** of a plan's
checklist, one `## Phase <n>` per phase. The plan is the execution ledger by
default: a sequentially executed plan keeps its `- [ ]` items in its own phases
and never gets a task file. Create one only to divide the work across concurrent
agents or disjoint scopes (each file with a `--stream <label>`); then that file
is the execution unit for its scope.

Companions, read in their situation: `tasks-handoff.md` (a second session,
claims, stale files) and `tasks-review.md` (closing a phase: verify-step,
review).

## Create

Create it with the CLI (path and frontmatter):

```bash
sdt context task add "<step>" [--phase <n>] [--stream <label>] [--plan <plan-file>]
```

`--phase <n>` places the step in that `## Phase` section (created when absent);
without it the step lands unphased. `--stream` selects a split file
(`<slug-plan>-<label>.md`). Timestamps: `sdt time iso` (RFC3339 UTC).

## Intent first

The chain and its gates are AGENTS.md HARD RULE 1: task file → plan →
analysis.

A task file is owned by its plan: `phases` lists the sections it covers (split
files only) and **inherits the plan's `objective`** — it never declares one.

**One home for the checklist.** In an unsplit plan the `- [ ]` steps live in the
plan's phases; in a split plan they live in the task file's `## Phase <n>`
sections, and the plan keeps only intent and approach. A plan phase and its task
section never restate the same checklist: moving an item into a task file is the
moment the plan stops being the ledger.

## Structure

```markdown
## Phase 1 — <goal>
- [ ] <step> <!-- c1 -->
- [ ] <step> <!-- c2 -->

## Phase 2 — <goal>
- [ ] <step> <!-- c3 -->

## Deviations             <!-- one item per deliberate departure from the plan -->

## Review                 <!-- file-level; written by `task review` on completion -->
### Findings             <!-- one item per finding; the verdict is the trailing token -->
### Gate                 <!-- appended by `agent gate --record`; a failed run says so -->
```

## Design section (study of the task objectives)

One short block per objective — the objective, the approach chosen, how it is
verified. The full style, architecture and dependency
contract lives in the plan (`plan.md`); do not duplicate it here.

## Checklist item detail

One self-contained action per item. A description indented below the checkbox
carries what one line cannot:

```markdown
- [ ] Refactor auth middleware to use a token pool
      File: `internal/auth/middleware.go` — replace per-request allocation;
      the public API must not change.
- [~] Add unit tests for the pool
      Must cover: concurrent access, exhaustion fallback.
- [ ] Update the migration script
      Depends on: the middleware refactor.
```

Keep it short: what changes, which files, what to verify. Each item gets a
trailing `<!-- c<N> -->` id when the CLI writes it. An item is a checklist line
plus its indented continuation lines; the id is written on the checklist line and
is **unique per document** (not per phase), so never add, edit, reorder or reuse
one by hand. A repeated id is a `sdt context lint` WARNING, and
`sdt context checklist backfill` renumbers duplicates.

## Frontmatter properties

| Property | Mandatory | Description |
|----------|-----------|-------------|
| kind | yes | Document type, fixed value `tasks`. |
| summary | yes | 1-2 sentences → index source. |
| phases | no | Sections this file covers (`[1, 2]`); split file only. |
| status | yes | `pending` / `in-progress` / `completed` / `archived`; `active` is legacy. |
| created | yes | ISO 8601 UTC (`sdt time iso`). |
| updated | yes | Written only by the CLI; a body edit uses `sdt context touch`. |
| links | yes | Relative path to the plan this file executes. |
| project | yes | Project id from `.sdt.yaml` (`<project-id>`). |
| agent, model, session | no | Provenance of the agent that last worked the file. |

## Rules

- **No H1 title** — AGENTS.md (document conventions).
- **CLI-only state** — never hand-edit `status:`, `updated:` or a checklist
  marker: `sdt context task <sub>` and `sdt context check <doc> <id> --state …`
  mutate them, `sdt context touch` refreshes a body edit.
- Markers: `[ ]` todo · `[~]` in-progress · `[x]` done · `[!]` blocked.
- Transitions: create → `pending`; `wip`/`block` → `in-progress`; `done` →
  `completed` when no `[ ]`/`[~]` remains, else `in-progress`; `archived` only
  on the user's explicit retirement.
- **One file per plan by default**; several only for disjoint scopes, each with
  `--stream` and `phases`. A plan phase with no matching section in any task
  file is a lint SUGGESTION.
- ~5-7 items per phase section; >10 is a SUGGESTION to split the phase.
- Task files are **living**, updated during execution.
- Every section done and the `## Review` block present → `completed`, and the
  linked plan is updated.

## Execution workflow

1. **Pick** — a file whose dependencies are satisfied (its plan and links). One
   at a time; concurrent sub-agents own different files.
2. **Take in charge** — `sdt context task wip <id> --phase <n>` before any work
   (sets `[~]`, flips the file `in-progress`, refreshes `updated`; add
   `agent`/`model`/`session`). Study the `## Design` objectives, filling them
   from the plan and analysis when missing. Log the take-in in the worklog.
3. **Execute** — work the section top to bottom, updating markers as you go. A
   deviation goes back into the file or the plan, never silently
   (`tasks-review.md`).
4. **Complete** — `[x]` every item, run the verify-step (`tasks-review.md`), then
   the next phase; set the file `completed` and update the plan.
5. **Commit gate** — see `instructions/git.md` for the procedure, the message
   format and the commit gate.

**Durable ledger.** Conversation memory does not survive compaction: the record
of progress lives in this file, the plan and the worklog, not short-lived
todos; narration between steps is one short line at most.

**Batched reads.** Read for breadth, not one file at a time: batch what a single
command can return; **Broad exploration goes to a sub-agent** — it takes back a
pointer plus the load-bearing lines; keep the original retrievable by naming its
files (`development.md`, *Context economy*). When this does not apply: a phase
that reads two known files needs no batch.

**Re-inject the hard rule at a boundary.** A rule that matters every phase loses
force in a long execution. **Restate it at each phase boundary** — at take-in,
and when a phase closes — in one line, pointing at the owning contract rather
than restating it. When this does not apply: a phase short enough to hold it
needs no re-injection.

**Rulings, not stalls.** Inside a plan, settle execution-level details
the plan already authorizes and record each as
`Ruling: <what was decided> — <why> — <cost if wrong>` in the file or worklog,
then keep going. A decision that changes scope, style, architecture or cost is
not a ruling — it is a question for the user (HARD RULE 7).

### The `>execute` trigger

`>execute` is the **workflow** command that drives this file. Its subject is a
`plan` and one of that plan's task files; it resolves the subject through the
working-context ladder (`context/commands/index.md`, *Working-context
resolution*) — explicit payload → exactly one active plan with an unfinished
task file → the plan under discussion → **ask**. Then:

- **Pick** — the file with no pending dependency; if several are eligible, apply
  the ladder and **ask** when it is not unique. Work one file at a time.
- **Take in** — `sdt context task wip <id> --phase <n>` before any work (step 2).
- **Execute / complete** — steps 3-4, including the verify-step.
- **Stop conditions** — exactly four; never invent work:
  - an operation the workflow reserves for the user — a commit, an AGENTS.md
    write, or any irreversible, destructive or security-sensitive action → stop
    and **ask**;
  - a side effect outside the task's scope — a merge, a push, a publish, or
    touching another work stream's files → stop and **ask**;
  - a broken or missing plan — no task file (point to `>tasks`) or a plan where
    every path forward is a guess → stop and **ask**;
  - an ambiguous or already-closed subject — more than one candidate plan/task,
    or a `completed` plan (do not reopen it silently) → list the candidates and
    **ask**.
